      "latency_ms": 51194.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1263,
      "output": "Water is a chemical substance that is essential for life on Earth. Here is a breakdown of what it is, how it is made, and why it is unique:\n\n### 1. Chemical Composition\nChemically, water is defined by the formula **$H_2O$**. This means that every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   **Two hydrogen atoms**\n*   **One oxygen atom**\n\nThese atoms are bonded together by a covalent bond, creating a stable molecule.\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is unique because it is the only common substance found naturally on Earth that can exist as a **solid, liquid, and gas** at the same time under standard conditions:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules move slowly and form a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we are most familiar with. The molecules are close together but can slide past one another, allowing water to flow.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules gain enough energy to break free from the liquid and float into the air.\n\n### 3. Polarity\nWater is a **polar molecule**. This means the molecule has a slight electrical charge.\n*   The oxygen end of the molecule is slightly **negative**.\n*   The hydrogen ends are slightly **positive**.\n\nBecause of this charge difference, water molecules are attracted to one another (a force called **hydrogen bonding**). This attraction is what gives water its high surface tension (which allows insects to walk on water) and its ability to act as a \"universal solvent\" (dissolving more substances than any other liquid).\n\n### 4. High Specific Heat Capacity\nWater is very good at storing heat. It takes a large amount of energy to raise the temperature of water and a large amount of energy to cool it down. This property helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the temperature of the human body stable.\n\n### 5. Importance\n*   **Life Support:** All known forms of life require water to survive. It is the primary component of cells, making up about 60% to 70% of the human body.\n*   **Habitat:** It covers about 71% of the Earth's surface and provides a home for countless plants and animals.\n*   **Weather:** Water plays a critical role in the water cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation), driving weather patterns around the globe."
